The house prompt gains a `noOpChecks` rule. Before trusting a green, name what
the no-op path looks like and confirm it is not this one: a skipped test that
returns Ok, a grep whose subject changed shape, a rollup field that reads
pending as passing, a verifier absent from the host it was meant to check.
Where a check can be skipped, the skip is an outcome something counts, never
silence. Where a check reads another tool, it reads that tool's state and not
its rendering. Coverage gets reported two ways: the share of subjects the
check reached, and the share that then passed.

## Why now

A sweep found eleven checks across two repos, all landed on one day, that
passed or failed for a reason unrelated to what they verify. Every one was
caught by a person who already knew the answer. None was caught by a gate.

The worst was not a blind spot but a correctness bug. `nox` fingerprints a
dirty tree by hashing `git diff --binary HEAD`. This repo sets
`diff.external difft`, so that command prints

```
Binary file modified (old: 2 KiB, new: 2 KiB)
```

which is byte-identical for two different binaries of the same size. The eval
cache and the NAR cache served the wrong tree's digest, under a module header
promising never a wrong hit.

## Why it is not folded into an existing rule

`validate` already says the terminal artifact is the outcome and not a
wrapper's zero exit. That is a wrapper lying about a check that ran. This is a
check that never ran, or a rendered summary read in place of the state behind
it. `classOverInstance` is about preferring a gate to a patch and says nothing
about whether the gate fires.

The last clause is the part that generalises past continuous integration. The
house ClickHouse rules already carry it: an unmatched `ASOF JOIN` row fills
with defaults rather than nulls, so a join that covered almost nothing reads
as a join that found zeros everywhere, and reporting one number instead of two
produced three confidently wrong measurements.
